# Modern Physics: Quanta, Atoms, and Nuclei

> Around 1900 physics changed gears: energy arrived in lumps called
> quanta, light began to behave like particles, and the nucleus gave up
> radioactivity, fission, and fusion. This lesson follows the quantum
> idea from Planck's equation to E = mc² and the electron microscope.

## 1. The Quantum Idea

[definition]
In 1900 Max Planck proposed that matter emits or absorbs energy not
continuously but in packets called **quanta**. The energy of one
quantum is

$E = h\nu$

where ν is the frequency of the radiation and **h** is Planck's
constant, $h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}$ J s.
[/definition]

Classical physics could not explain why a hot body's colour shifts with
temperature (red-hot, then white-hot) or why light below a certain
frequency frees no electrons at all — Planck's lumps could. Energy is
*quantised*: charges sit on the rungs of an energy ladder, not on a
smooth ramp.

## 2. The Photoelectric Effect

[definition]
The **photoelectric effect** is the ejection of electrons from a metal
surface when light of sufficiently high frequency falls on it. Einstein
explained it in 1905 with **photons**: light of frequency ν delivers
energy in quanta $h\nu$, one photon meeting one electron. The work won
him the Nobel Prize, not relativity.
[/definition]

| Observation | Photon answer |
| --- | --- |
| No emission below a threshold frequency, however bright the light | a photon must carry at least the work function φ |
| Emission is instant even in feeble light | one photon either has enough energy or does not |
| Maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency, not intensity | $K_{max} = h\nu - \phi$ |
| Brighter light gives more electrons at the same speed | more photons, each delivering hν |

The **work function** φ is the minimum energy needed to free an
electron from the metal; the **threshold frequency** follows from
$\nu_{0} = \phi/h$.

[example]
Light of frequency $1.0 \times 10^{15}$ Hz strikes a metal whose work
function is 2.0 eV. The photon energy is $h\nu = 6.63 \times 10^{-19}$ J
≈ 4.15 eV, so $K_{max} = 4.15 - 2.0 = 2.15$ eV. Dimming the light
frees fewer electrons — never slower ones.
[/example]

[tip]
Exams love the swap: intensity controls the *number* of photoelectrons,
frequency controls their *maximum kinetic energy*. Reversing these two
is the classic mistake.
[/tip]

## 3. Bohr Model of the Atom

Rutherford's nuclear atom had a fatal flaw: an orbiting electron should
radiate energy continuously and spiral into the nucleus in a blink. In
1913 Niels Bohr patched it with quantum rules:

1. Electrons move only in certain fixed orbits (stationary states) and
   do not radiate while in them.
2. Each orbit carries a definite energy; for hydrogen
   $E_{n} = -13.6/n^{2}$ eV.
3. Radiation is emitted or absorbed only in a jump between levels:
   $h\nu = E_{2} - E_{1}$.
4. The angular momentum of an orbit is quantised: $mvr = nh/2\pi$.

[example]
The visible Balmer lines of hydrogen come from jumps that *end* on the
n = 2 level. The fall from n = 3 to n = 2 emits the red H-alpha line at
656 nm — the fingerprint every hydrogen discharge lamp shows.
[/example]

Bohr's model triumphed for hydrogen but faltered on multi-electron
atoms and fine structure — full quantum mechanics later went deeper.

## 4. Atomic Number, Isotopes, and Isobars

@diagram tree id="atom-structure" layout="tree" aria="Structure of the atom with nucleus and electrons"
  "Atom" -> "Nucleus (protons and neutrons)"
  "Atom" -> "Electrons in shells"
  "Nucleus (protons and neutrons)" -> "Protons: charge +1"
  "Nucleus (protons and neutrons)" -> "Neutrons: neutral"
@/diagram

| Quantity | Symbol | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Atomic number | Z | number of protons — fixes the element's identity |
| Mass number | A | protons plus neutrons |
| Neutron number | N | A − Z |

[definition]
**Isotopes** share the same Z but differ in A — the same element with
different neutron counts. **Isobars** share the same A but differ in Z
— different elements of equal mass.
[/definition]

| Nuclide | Nucleus | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Protium ¹H | 1 proton | ordinary hydrogen |
| Deuterium ²H | proton + neutron | heavy water (D₂O), a reactor moderator |
| Tritium ³H | proton + 2 neutrons | tracer and fusion fuel |
| Carbon-12 | 6p + 6n | the standard for atomic masses |
| Carbon-14 | 6p + 8n | radiocarbon dating |

## 5. Radioactivity: Alpha, Beta, Gamma

[definition]
**Radioactivity** is the spontaneous disintegration of unstable nuclei
with emission of particles or radiation. Becquerel discovered it in
1896 when uranium salts fogged an unexposed photographic plate; Marie
and Pierre Curie went on to isolate polonium and radium.
[/definition]

| Property | Alpha (α) | Beta (β) | Gamma (γ) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Nature | helium nucleus ⁴₂He | fast electron | high-energy photon |
| Charge | +2 | −1 | 0 |
| Penetration | stopped by paper | stopped by a few mm of aluminium | reduced by thick lead |
| Effect on nucleus | A − 4, Z − 2 | A unchanged, Z + 1 | A and Z unchanged |
| Ionising power | strongest | moderate | weakest |

@diagram tree id="decay-modes" layout="tree" aria="Three decay modes and their nuclear changes"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Alpha: A-4, Z-2"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Beta: A same, Z+1"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Gamma: no nuclear change"
@/diagram

[warning]
Alpha is the *most* ionising yet the *least* penetrating; gamma is the
reverse. Students swap these constantly. Rutherford separated the three
with electric and magnetic fields: alpha bends one way, beta the other,
gamma not at all.
[/warning]

## 6. Half-Life

[definition]
The **half-life** (t½) is the time in which half the nuclei of a sample
decay. Decay is statistical: no one can say when a *particular* nucleus
will break, only how fast the population thins out.
[/definition]

@chart line title="Fraction of sample remaining vs half-lives elapsed (%)"
labels:
0
1
2
3
4

data:
100
50
25
12.5
6.25
@/chart

[example]
An 80 g sample has a half-life of 5 days. After 15 days — three
half-lives — only 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 g remains. After 25 days, five
half-lives, just 2.5 g survives.
[/example]

Carbon-14 (t½ = 5,730 years) dates once-living matter; iodine-131 with
its 8-day half-life suits short medical doses that fade quickly.

## 7. E = mc²: Fission and Fusion

@math label="eq:mass-energy"
E = mc^{2}
@/math

Einstein's mass–energy equivalence: a **mass defect** m — the missing
mass when nucleons bind into a nucleus — appears as energy with the
enormous conversion factor c². Converting just 1 g of matter releases
about 9 × 10¹³ J, dwarfing every chemical reaction.

| | Fission | Fusion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| What happens | a heavy nucleus splits into two | light nuclei merge into one |
| Trigger | neutron absorption | extreme temperature and pressure |
| Fuel | U-235, Pu-239 | hydrogen isotopes (D, T) |
| Where | reactors, atomic bombs | the Sun and stars, hydrogen bomb |
| Waste | long-lived radioactive waste | mainly helium — cleaner |
| Control | steady in reactors (moderators, control rods) | not yet tamed on Earth (ITER) |

[example]
A neutron splits ²³⁵U into barium and krypton plus three fresh
neutrons, which can split further nuclei — a **chain reaction**. In a
reactor, moderators (water, heavy water) slow the neutrons and control
rods (cadmium, boron) absorb the excess; a bomb is the same reaction
left uncontrolled.
[/example]

## 8. Wave–Particle Duality and Applications

De Broglie flipped the coin in 1924: if waves act like particles,
particles should act like waves, with wavelength

$\lambda = h/p$

Electrons diffract exactly so — the principle of the **electron
microscope**, which resolves far finer detail than light because an
electron's wavelength is thousands of times smaller.

| Application | Physics it uses |
| --- | --- |
| Radiocarbon dating | carbon-14 half-life |
| Cancer radiotherapy | gamma beams from Co-60 |
| Smoke detectors | alpha particles from americium-241 |
| Sterilising medical tools | gamma radiation |
| Nuclear power | controlled fission plus E = mc² |
| Electron microscope | de Broglie waves |

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Quantum energy | E = hν; h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s |
| Photoelectric | Kmax = hν − φ; intensity → number, frequency → energy |
| Bohr levels | En = −13.6/n² eV for hydrogen |
| Isotopes and isobars | same Z vs same A |
| Alpha, beta, gamma | A−4 Z−2 / Z+1 / no nuclear change |
| Half-life | half the sample decays each t½ |
| Mass–energy | E = mc²; mass defect → energy |
| Fission vs fusion | heavy splits vs light merges |
| Duality | matter waves: λ = h/p |
